    {
      "short": "Shard it",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "title": "Sharding — and the hot-partition trap",
      "body": [
        "Replication copies the same data; <span class=\"term\">sharding</span> <em>splits</em> it. Each shard owns a slice of the keyspace and takes a slice of the writes — the only way past a single primary's write ceiling.",
        "But sharding is only as good as the <span class=\"term\">shard key</span>. A monotonic key routes every new write to one shard. A hashed key spreads load evenly — and makes range queries <span class=\"term\">scatter-gather</span>."
      ],
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Hot partitions hide until a hot <em>entity</em> shows up. Design for your most viral user, not your average one."
      },
      "metrics": {
